---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import TerminalCast from '@/components/TerminalCast.astro'

const probeYaml = `livenessProbe:
  httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: 4317 }
  periodSeconds: 10
readinessProbe:
  httpGet: { path: /readyz, port: 4317 }
  periodSeconds: 5

# SIGTERM is handled, so give in-flight ingest time to finish.
terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 30`

const scrape = `scrape_configs:
  - job_name: mcpd
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['mcpd:4317']`
---

<Doc
  title="Running it"
  description="Operating mcpd in production: liveness and readiness probes, the Prometheus metrics it exports, graceful shutdown, backing up the SQLite file, upgrades, and where a single instance stops."
  lede="mcpd is one process holding one file. That makes most of operating it short, and it makes the few things that matter worth knowing exactly."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>What is on the port</h2>
  <p>
    One address serves everything: OTLP ingest at <code>/v1/traces</code>,
    <code>/v1/logs</code> and <code>/v1/metrics</code>, native ingest at
    <code>/v1/ingest</code>, the query API under <code>/v1/api</code>, the
    operator interface at <code>/</code>, and the three operational endpoints
    below. There is no sidecar and no reverse proxy to configure, which is most
    of why the deployment fits on one line.
  </p>
  <p>
    The interface is compiled into the binary, so there is no static file server
    and no second volume mount. Ingest carries the large body limit and no CORS.
    The query API carries CORS, so an interface served from another origin can
    read it.
  </p>

  <h2>/healthz and /readyz are not the same question</h2>
  <p>
    They are separate because they answer to different consequences, and wiring
    both to the same handler is a common way to build a restart loop.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Endpoint</th>
          <th>Touches storage</th>
          <th>Answers</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>GET /healthz</code></td>
          <td>No</td>
          <td>Always <code>200</code> while the process is serving</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>GET /readyz</code></td>
          <td>Yes, one read of each store</td>
          <td><code>200</code> when the signal store and the run store answer, <code>503</code> when either does not</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <code>/healthz</code> is liveness. It deliberately never touches storage: a
    slow or locked database must not get the process killed and restarted,
    because restarting it does not fix a disk and the restart loop costs you the
    in-flight batches as well.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>/readyz</code> is readiness. It runs one trivial query against a
    reader connection, so an instance that cannot serve stops being sent
    traffic. When storage is unreachable it answers <code>503</code> with a
    <code>status</code> of <code>degraded</code> and a <code>detail</code>
    naming the failure, rather than a bare status code you then have to
    investigate blind.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both bodies carry the same fields: <code>status</code>,
    <code>version</code>, <code>spec_version</code>,
    <code>uptime_seconds</code>, <code>storage</code>, and
    <code>detail</code> when something is wrong. <code>spec_version</code> is
    the data-model version this build implements, which is how a client can
    refuse to talk to a server it does not understand.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="probing a healthy instance"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'curl -s localhost:4317/healthz' },
      { out: '{"status":"ok","version":"0.1.0","spec_version":"1.0.0",' },
      { out: ' "uptime_seconds":86400,"storage":"sqlite"}', tone: 'ok' },
      { cmd: 'curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\\n" localhost:4317/readyz' },
      { out: '200', tone: 'ok' },
      { cmd: 'curl -s localhost:4317/metrics | head -4' },
      { out: '# HELP mcpd_signals_accepted_total Signals written to storage.' },
      { out: '# TYPE mcpd_signals_accepted_total counter' },
      { out: 'mcpd_signals_accepted_total 184203' },
      { out: '# HELP mcpd_signals_rejected_total Signals refused, with a reason returned to the sender.' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Neither probe requires a key, and neither does <code>/metrics</code>. If
    that is not acceptable in your network, restrict them at your ingress.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="kubernetes"
    samples={[{ lang: 'curl', label: 'Pod spec', shiki: 'yaml', code: probeYaml }]}
  />

  <h2>Metrics</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd exports its own counters at <code>/metrics</code> in Prometheus text
    exposition format. They are not behind a feature flag: an observability tool
    that cannot be observed is a liability.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Metric</th>
          <th>Type</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_signals_accepted_total</code></td>
          <td>counter</td>
          <td>Signals written to storage</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_signals_rejected_total</code></td>
          <td>counter</td>
          <td>Signals refused, with a reason returned to the sender</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_batches_duplicate_total</code></td>
          <td>counter</td>
          <td>Batches recognised as a replay and skipped</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_traces_sampled_out_total</code></td>
          <td>counter</td>
          <td>Traces the sampling policy discarded</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_signals_over_quota_total</code></td>
          <td>counter</td>
          <td>Signals refused for exceeding a project's per-minute ceiling</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_signals_unbounded_total</code></td>
          <td>counter</td>
          <td>
            Signals that reached the ceiling step for a project no ceiling
            covers
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_ingest_requests_total</code></td>
          <td>counter</td>
          <td>Ingest requests handled</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_query_requests_total</code></td>
          <td>counter</td>
          <td>Query requests handled</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_request_errors_total</code></td>
          <td>counter</td>
          <td>Requests that failed with a server error</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_audit_write_failures_total</code></td>
          <td>counter</td>
          <td>
            Audit rows that could not be written; the request they describe
            still succeeded
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_uptime_seconds</code></td>
          <td>gauge</td>
          <td>Seconds since this process started</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_model_input_tokens_total</code>, <code>mcpd_model_output_tokens_total</code></td>
          <td>counter</td>
          <td>
            Tokens on stored spans, labelled by <code>provider</code> and
            <code>model</code>. At most 200 series; spans past that are counted
            under <code>other</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>mcpd_model_cost_total</code></td>
          <td>counter</td>
          <td>Priced cost of stored spans, by provider, model and <code>currency</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Every counter is process-lifetime and resets to zero on restart, so alert on
    a rate rather than a level. The two worth watching are
    <code>mcpd_signals_rejected_total</code>, which means senders are being told
    no and probably are not reading the reply, and
    <code>mcpd_request_errors_total</code>, which is mcpd's own fault by
    definition: a 4xx is the caller's and is never counted there.
  </p>
  <p>
    A rising <code>mcpd_traces_sampled_out_total</code> is not a fault. It is
    the number that explains a gap in the interface when somebody asks why their
    run is missing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>mcpd_signals_over_quota_total</code> is the same shape of number for
    the other reason a signal does not land: a project hit its per-minute
    ceiling. Those refusals are also counted in
    <code>mcpd_signals_rejected_total</code>, so watch this one to tell a fleet
    that is being limited apart from a fleet that is sending malformed data. See
    <a href="/docs/configuration" class="link">Configuration</a> for what the
    ceiling covers.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>mcpd_signals_unbounded_total</code> is the opposite number: signals
    that were kept because no ceiling covered the project they named. Ceilings
    written only under <code>projects</code> bound the projects they name, so a
    sender that names anything else walks past them. Those signals are counted
    rather than refused, because refusing a project nobody wrote down would drop
    the data of a deployment that upgraded without changing its configuration.
    Non-zero means set a top-level <code>--quota-per-minute</code> beside the
    per-project ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    The ceiling covers every door a row can come in through: the four ingest
    endpoints, the single-record score and feedback endpoints, and
    <code>POST /v1/api/score</code>, whose scores are computed and then charged
    against the same counter before they are stored. What does not fit under
    the ceiling there is reported per score in the response's
    <code>skipped</code> list rather than written past it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>mcpd_audit_write_failures_total</code> counts audit rows that could
    not be written. The request each row described still succeeded and still
    returned its data, which is the point: an audit write never fails the read
    it describes. Non-zero therefore means reads of stored payloads happened
    that the audit log cannot account for, so treat it as an integrity signal
    rather than an availability one.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="prometheus"
    samples={[{ lang: 'curl', label: 'prometheus.yml', shiki: 'yaml', code: scrape }]}
  />

  <h2>Shutdown</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd handles <code>SIGTERM</code> and <code>SIGINT</code>. On either, it
    stops accepting new connections and lets in-flight requests finish before
    the process exits.
  </p>
  <p>
    Handling <code>SIGTERM</code> specifically is what makes this work under an
    orchestrator. Kubernetes sends <code>SIGTERM</code> and waits out the grace
    period before <code>SIGKILL</code>; a container that only listens for
    <code>SIGINT</code> is killed rather than stopped, and whatever batch was
    mid-transaction is lost.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="a clean stop"
    lines={[
      { out: 'mcpd 0.1.0 listening on http://0.0.0.0:4317' },
      { out: '  storage     sqlite at /data/mcpd.db' },
      { out: 'retention removed 12480 rows older than 30 days', tone: 'note' },
      { out: 'received termination signal', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'shutting down', tone: 'ok' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    What mcpd cannot save on the way out is anything still sitting in a sender's
    queue. The SDKs hold spans in memory and export them on a worker, so a
    process that exits without calling <code>shutdown()</code> loses whatever
    had not gone yet. That is the agent's shutdown to get right, not the
    daemon's.
  </p>

  <h2>Backups</h2>
  <p>
    The whole store is one SQLite file at <code>--data</code>. It runs in WAL
    mode, so on disk you will find <code>mcpd.db</code> alongside
    <code>mcpd.db-wal</code> and <code>mcpd.db-shm</code>. Copying the
    <code>.db</code> on its own while the process is running gives you a file
    that is missing everything still in the write-ahead log.
  </p>
  <p>
    Take the backup through SQLite, which reads a consistent snapshot while
    mcpd keeps writing:
  </p>

  <Command
    class="my-6"
    label="hot backup"
    command={`sqlite3 /data/mcpd.db ".backup '/backup/mcpd-$(date +%F).db'"`}
  />

  <p>
    Stopping the process first and copying all three files is equally valid and
    needs no <code>sqlite3</code> binary in the image. There is no
    <code>mcpd backup</code> subcommand in this build, and no scheduled backup:
    this is your snapshot tooling's job, and pretending otherwise would put a
    half-built one in the critical path.
  </p>
  <p>
    Retention deletes rows but does not shrink the file. SQLite keeps the freed
    pages and reuses them, so disk usage plateaus rather than falling after a
    large delete. Run <code>VACUUM</code> against a stopped instance if you
    actually need the space back.
  </p>

  <h3>Restore</h3>
  <p>
    Putting a backup back is four steps, and the order matters because of the
    write-ahead log.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Stop the daemon. A restore under a running process is a second writer on
      the same file.
    </li>
    <li>Copy the backup over the file at <code>--data</code>.</li>
    <li>
      Delete the <code>-wal</code> and <code>-shm</code> files beside it. They
      belong to the database you replaced, and left in place they would be
      replayed into the one you restored.
    </li>
    <li>
      Start the daemon, and confirm it is reading the restored file:
      <code>/readyz</code> answers 200, and a trace list against the project
      returns the runs the backup held.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="restore, run against a demo store"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'kill $(cat mcpd.pid) && cp /backup/mcpd-2026-09-01.db /data/mcpd.db' },
      { cmd: 'rm -f /data/mcpd.db-wal /data/mcpd.db-shm' },
      { cmd: 'mcpd --data /data/mcpd.db &' },
      { cmd: 'curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\\n" localhost:4317/readyz' },
      { out: '200', tone: 'ok' },
      { cmd: 'curl -s "localhost:4317/v1/api/traces?project=default&limit=500" | jq ".traces | length"' },
      { out: '2', tone: 'ok' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Upgrades</h2>
  <p>
    Replace the binary or the image tag and start it again. The schema is
    applied at startup, every time, and is written so that running it against an
    up-to-date file changes nothing. The applied version is recorded in a
    <code>schema_version</code> table inside the same file, and this build is at
    version 2.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two things to keep in mind. Roll forward, not back: an older binary is not
    promised to read a newer file, so take the backup first. And check
    <code>spec_version</code> from <code>/healthz</code> after the upgrade if
    you pin SDK versions, because that is the number a client compares against.
  </p>

  <h2>Where one instance stops</h2>
  <p>
    Being honest about this is more useful than a number we have not measured.
    We publish no throughput figures, because none exist that we could stand
    behind.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>One writer.</strong> The SQLite store keeps a single write
      connection behind a mutex and four read connections. WAL mode means a
      dashboard query never blocks ingest, which is the failure that would
      matter, but all writes are serialised through that one connection.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One instance per file.</strong> Two mcpd processes pointed at the
      same file is not a supported deployment. Run it as a single replica with
      a volume, not as a horizontally scaled service.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Both limits above are the embedded store's.</strong> They stop
      applying on ClickHouse, which is compiled in and covered below.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Payloads are large.</strong> Prompts and completions are
      kilobytes, not bytes, and they are what makes mcpd worth running. Storage
      grows with text volume far faster than with span count, so measure with
      your own payloads rather than with a span-per-second figure.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Retention covers the projects you wrote down.</strong> The hourly
      pass deletes from every project in the <code>projects</code> block, each
      on its own <code>retention_days</code> where it sets one and on the global
      figure where it does not, plus the project named by
      <code>--project</code>. A project a sender invented that appears in
      neither place has no window and is never trimmed, which is worth knowing
      before you rely on retention as a privacy control.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When volume outgrows the instance, the levers in order of how much they cost
    you are: <code>--retention-days</code>, which bounds the file;
    <code>--sample</code>, which bounds the inflow and is deterministic so a
    kept trace is kept whole; and moving to ClickHouse, which removes the single
    writer and the single replica together. See
    <a href="/docs/sampling" class="link">Sampling</a> and
    <a href="/docs/privacy" class="link">Redaction and retention</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Postgres, when you already run one</h2>
  <p>
    Point mcpd at a Postgres connection string and it stores there instead of
    the embedded file. The schema is created on start.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="my-6"
    title="store into Postgres"
    command="mcpd serve --postgres-url postgres://mcpd:$PGPASSWORD@db:5432/mcpd"
  />

  <p>
    This removes the same two limits ClickHouse does. Writes no longer pass
    through a single connection, so mcpd runs as several replicas behind one
    service, and it does so on a database most teams already operate, back up
    and monitor.
  </p>
  <p>
    Migration takes a Postgres advisory lock and is skipped entirely when the
    schema is already current. Several replicas starting at once is therefore
    safe, and a rolling deploy does not take an exclusive lock on tables that
    other replicas are writing to.
  </p>
  <p>
    Full-text search uses a generated <code>tsvector</code> column with a GIN
    index, in the <code>simple</code> configuration: it lower-cases and splits
    on word boundaries but does not stem, so searching a prompt for the literal
    token you typed does what you expect.
  </p>
  <p>
    Choose ClickHouse over Postgres when retention is measured in billions of
    spans. Below that, the database you already run is the better answer.
  </p>

  <h2>ClickHouse, when the file is no longer the right shape</h2>
  <p>
    Point mcpd at a ClickHouse HTTP endpoint and it stores there instead of the
    embedded file. The schema is created on start, so there is nothing to apply
    by hand.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="my-6"
    title="store into ClickHouse"
    command={`mcpd serve \\
  --clickhouse-url http://clickhouse:8123 \\
  --clickhouse-database mcpd \\
  --clickhouse-user default --clickhouse-password $CH_PASSWORD`}
  />

  <p>
    Both constraints above lift at once. Writes no longer pass through a single
    connection, so mcpd runs as several replicas behind one service, and spans
    land in a <code>MergeTree</code> built for the shape this data actually has:
    far more writing than reading, and queries that scan a time range.
  </p>
  <p>
    Choose it when retention is measured in months of production traffic rather
    than in a laptop's disk. On anything smaller the embedded file is faster to
    run, faster to back up, and one fewer thing to operate. Keep the file until
    it hurts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Traces are a <code>ReplacingMergeTree</code>, because a trace projection is
    recomputed as its spans arrive and the last write is the correct one. Spans
    arriving out of order, retried, or updated in place therefore converge on
    the right answer rather than accumulating duplicates.
  </p>
  <p>
    One guarantee is weaker here than on the other two backends, and it is the
    one you may have to certify. Deletion on ClickHouse is an asynchronous
    mutation, so an erasure request is accepted and applied afterwards. SQLite
    and Postgres delete inside the transaction that answered. The reply says
    which of the two happened, in <code>deletes_pending</code>. See
    <a href="/docs/privacy" class="link">Redaction and retention</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Checking a deployment</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mcpd doctor</code> reads the configuration and the store and reports
    what is wrong, naming the flag that fixes each thing. It exists because the
    failures that actually bite are quiet ones: nothing crashes, and you find
    out weeks later.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="my-6"
    title="check a deployment without contacting anything but the store"
    command="mcpd doctor --data /data/mcpd.db --offline"
    primaryLabel="Offline"
    secondary={[
      { label: 'Without writing', command: 'mcpd doctor --data /data/mcpd.db --offline --read-only' },
      { label: 'A longer window', command: 'mcpd doctor --data /data/mcpd.db --window-days 30' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Three flags are the command's own. <code>--offline</code> contacts nothing
    but the store, so the forward endpoint is reported as skipped rather than
    checked. <code>--read-only</code> writes nothing, so the storage check
    proves the store can be read and not that it can be written, and says so.
    <code>--window-days</code> is how many days of history the activity, clock
    and price-book checks read, seven unless you say otherwise, between 1 and
    36525. Everything else it takes is the daemon's own configuration, so it
    grades the invocation you would start.
  </p>
  <p>
    Twelve groups are graded, in this order, and the report ends with a count of
    each level:
  </p>

  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Group</th><th>What it grades</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>storage</code></td>
          <td>The store answers a ping, and a probe span survives a write, a read and a delete</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>runs</code></td>
          <td>The run store answers, how many definition versions and running runs it holds, and whether the run surface needs a key on an address other people can reach. Read only: the doctor never claims a step or fires a deadline</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>keys</code></td>
          <td>Every scope, with its inheritance made explicit, so the case where no admin key is set and every read key therefore also deletes is on the page rather than in the documentation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>identity</code></td>
          <td>The audit subject header, and whether the range permitted to set it is narrow enough to believe</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>projects</code></td>
          <td>Configured projects against projects that hold runs. One that has never received a span is almost always a typo in a name</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>retention</code></td>
          <td>How far back each project's data goes, and whether anything will ever delete it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>limits</code></td>
          <td>The per-project ingest ceilings and the sampling fractions</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>forwarding</code></td>
          <td>The forward endpoint, by POSTing an OTLP batch with no spans in it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>judge</code></td>
          <td>The judge block, as configuration only</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>monitors</code></td>
          <td>Monitors and objectives, and the signals they read</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>clock</code></td>
          <td>Runs from both ends of each project's history against this machine's clock, because a sender whose clock runs behind sorts underneath every honest run and would never appear on a page of the newest ones</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>pricing</code></td>
          <td>Recent model spans against the price book, so a model nothing can price is named</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    <code>identity</code> and <code>limits</code> are the two most recently
    added, and both were ungraded for the same reason: nothing about them
    crashes. An audit log with no subject records what was done and never who
    did it. A sampling fraction outside 0.0 to 1.0 is refused for
    <code>--sample</code> and was never checked in a project block, so a project
    could carry one and the daemon would run it. A ceiling set per project with
    no top-level ceiling beside it leaves every project nobody wrote down with
    no ceiling at all, and the sender chooses the project name.
  </p>

  <h3>Skipped is not ok</h3>
  <p>
    A check that was not run says <strong>skipped</strong>, never ok. That
    distinction is the point of the command: a subsystem missing from the
    configuration you pointed it at is not a subsystem in good health, and
    reporting the two the same way would make doctor most confident exactly when
    it had been handed the wrong file. The summary line counts the skipped
    checks separately and says so in words, so a run that verified two of its
    fourteen findings cannot read as a clean bill of health.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="how a run against a fresh deployment ends"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'mcpd doctor --data /data/mcpd.db | tail -4' },
      { out: '2 ok, 9 skipped, 2 warnings, 1 fault', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'A skipped check proved nothing: it was not run, or there was nothing there' },
      { out: 'to run it against. Only the 2 ok were verified.' },
      { out: 'A fault is something broken or open. Exiting 2.', tone: 'warn' },
    ]}
  />

  <h3>A busy store is not a broken one</h3>
  <p>
    The storage probe is the only check that needs the write lock. Everything
    below it reads, and readers do not queue behind a writer. A probe write that
    loses the race for that lock is reported as <strong>skipped</strong>: it is
    attempted three times, and if all three wait for the lock and give up, the
    report says the write was not checked and why.
  </p>
  <p>
    This matters because the embedded store takes one writer, and a deployment
    under real ingest holds that lock almost continuously. Reporting contention
    as a fault made the command fail a deploy precisely when the deployment was
    healthy and busy. The failures the probe exists to catch each name
    themselves and none of them says "locked": a read-only mount, a full disk
    and a role without <code>INSERT</code> are still faults, and so is a corrupt
    lock, which says "locking protocol" and is real damage.
  </p>

  <h3>The exit code</h3>
  <p>
    It exits non-zero on a fault, so it can gate a deploy. A warning is not a
    fault: it exits zero and expects a person to read it. One configuration is a
    fault rather than a warning, and it is worth knowing before you wire this
    into a pipeline: an open read scope on a non-loopback address, which hands
    every stored prompt to anyone who can reach the port. An open ingest scope
    on the same address stays a warning, because junk rows are what retention
    removes and disclosure cannot be taken back. A trusted identity range that
    contains the whole internet is graded the same way, for the same reason: any
    address could then set the header and be recorded as whoever it names.
  </p>

  <h3>What runs without the command</h3>
  <p>
    You have to know <code>mcpd doctor</code> exists to run it, and none of the
    mistakes it finds announces itself, so the deployment carrying one is
    usually the deployment nobody pointed it at. The groups that read only the
    configuration therefore run on every start: <code>keys</code>,
    <code>identity</code>, <code>limits</code> and <code>judge</code>. They need
    no store round trip and no network, so a start pays microseconds for them.
    The other eight stay in the command, because each reads the store or
    contacts the forward endpoint, and a start must wait on neither.
  </p>
  <p>
    A fault found at startup stops the daemon; a warning is logged and it
    carries on. What separates them is whether the configuration asks for
    something that cannot work: a key entry that is empty or has whitespace at
    an end, a trusted range holding the whole internet, a project
    <code>sample</code> outside 0.0 to 1.0, or a judge that can never answer.
    Each of those is a feature you turned on that silently does nothing, which
    is worse than one that fails, because nobody goes looking for it. A short
    key, a key two projects share, a ceiling smaller than one batch: those work,
    and may be what you meant, so they are logged and never fatal.
  </p>
  <p>
    The openness mcpd ships with is the exception. An open ingest or read scope
    on a non-loopback address is a fault to <code>mcpd doctor</code>, which
    gates a deploy, and a warning at startup, which does not. Refusing there
    would mean a first run that never starts.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>mcpd config</code> and <code>mcpd doctor</code> themselves run against
    a configuration a start would refuse. They are the two commands that exist
    to explain a bad one, so neither can be the command that will not run.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="a judge that could never have produced a score"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'mcpd --config-file /etc/mcpd/config.json' },
      { out: 'Error: the configuration cannot do what it says, so mcpd will', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'not start:', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '  judge: the judge at https://api.example.com/v1 has no criteria,', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '  so every judged scorer declines with `is not a configured', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '  criterion` and no judged score is ever produced. Add entries', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '  under `judge.criteria` in --config-file.', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'Fix the lines above, or run `mcpd doctor` for the whole report.', tone: 'warn' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>The settings to check before it is reachable</h2>
  <p>
    When it binds a non-loopback address, mcpd logs a warning at startup for
    each surface open to a caller that should not reach it, and each warning
    names the flags to set. It warns rather than refusing to start, because
    refusing would break the path from nothing to a first trace. It is still a
    warning about a real hole: an open read API hands every stored prompt to
    anyone who can reach the port.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="my-6"
    command="mcpd --data /data/mcpd.db --ingest-key $INGEST_KEY --read-key $READ_KEY --execute-key $EXECUTE_KEY --worker-key $WORKER_KEY --admin-key $ADMIN_KEY --retention-days 30"
  />

  <p>
    Without the last three keys, the ingest key and the read key also reach the
    run surface, and the read key also deletes. Full detail on every key, and on
    everything else you can set, is in
    <a href="/docs/configuration" class="link">Configuration</a>.
  </p>
</Doc>
